^Springwater
^screenplay
^title_page
^title
THE FIRST SWIM
^title/
^written_by
Original scene in the manner of a classic suspense opening
^written_by/
^title_page/

^act1
^scene
^slugline
EXT. BEACH - NIGHT
^slugline/

^action
A driftwood fire. Sparks lift and die against the dark.
^action/

^action
Teenagers ring the flames. Someone works a harmonica, badly, and no one minds. Bottles pass hand to hand. The ocean is a black field beyond the light, heard and not seen.
^action/

^action
A YOUNG MAN watches a GIRL across the fire. She is already watching the water.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
YOUNG MAN
^character/
^lines
I'm going in. You coming?
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
She is already up. Already running.
^action/
^scene/

^scene
^slugline
EXT. DUNES - CONTINUOUS
^slugline/

^action
They run through the beach grass, peeling clothing as they go, leaving it where it falls. He is laughing. He has had too much to drink and his feet know it.
^action/

^action
She reaches the open sand ahead of him and does not slow.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
YOUNG MAN
^character/
^parenthetical
(behind her, fading)
^parenthetical/
^lines
Wait. Hey. Wait up.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
He makes it to the waterline and no farther. He sits down hard in the cold sand, grinning, done. The world tips pleasantly around him.
^action/
^scene/

^scene
^slugline
EXT. OCEAN - NIGHT
^slugline/

^action
The GIRL wades out and gives herself to the water. She swims with long, easy strokes toward the marker bell, pale against the dark surface, the only moving thing for a hundred yards.
^action/

^action
Behind her, the beach is a thin orange line. Ahead, nothing. She rolls onto her back and floats, breathing, the stars wheeling slow above her.
^action/

^action
Below, the water holds its silence.
^action/

^action
Something brushes her foot.
^action/

^action
She stops. Treads water. Listens to a sea that gives nothing back.
^action/

^action
Then she is pulled down to the shoulders in one hard motion, as if the ocean has taken hold of her, and dragged sideways across the surface, fast, fighting.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
GIRL
^character/
^lines
Oh God. Oh God, please.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
She catches the bell line and clings to it. The bell tolls, flat and stupid in the dark. For a moment she is still.
^action/

^action
The line goes slack.
^action/

^action
She is taken under. The surface closes. The bell rings once more on its own swing and is quiet.
^action/
^scene/

^scene
^slugline
EXT. BEACH - DAWN
^slugline/

^action
The young man lies asleep in the sand where he fell, one arm flung toward the water.
^action/

^action
The tide has turned. It reaches for the clothes they left and carries them, piece by piece, a little way out.
^action/
^scene/

^transition
SMASH CUT TO:
^transition/
